\refdeft{even subalgebra of a Clifford algebra}{§8.3, Theorem 8.5 and proof, p. 104}{wieser2024formalizing}{
\lean{CliffordAlgebra.even}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative ring, #{V} an #{R}-module and
#{Q:V\to R} a \vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004}. Write #{\Cl(Q)} for its
\vocabk{Clifford algebra}{ca-0006}, with canonical #{R}-linear
\vocabk{Clifford map}{ca-000A} #{\iota:V\to\Cl(Q)}.}

\p{The \newvocab{even subalgebra}, also called the
\newvocab{even Clifford algebra}, is the #{R}-linear span of all
even words:
##{
\Cl^+(Q)=\operatorname{span}_R
\{\iota(v_1)\cdots\iota(v_{2k})\}.
}
Here #{k} ranges over all nonnegative integers and each #{v_i} ranges over
#{V}; the word for #{k=0} is the empty product #{1}. Thus an
\newvocab{even element} is a finite #{R}-linear combination of such words,
including scalar multiples of #{1}.}

\p{The product of two even words is an even word. Distributivity therefore
makes this span closed under multiplication, and the empty word supplies
the unit. Hence it is an #{R}-subalgebra of #{\Cl(Q)}. In Mathlib its
underlying submodule is \leanref{CliffordAlgebra.evenOdd} #{Q\,0},
as stated by \leanref{CliffordAlgebra.even_toSubmodule}.
The induction principle \leanref{CliffordAlgebra.even_induction}
proves a property of every even element from its scalar case and
preservation under addition and left multiplication by pairs of
Clifford generators.}
}
